Player: I should speak with the villagers first and let everyone know about the council meeting—then we can gather here to decide together. [#hint=Quest 3: announce the meeting at the village entrance]
=> END

~ chapter1_locked_finish_council_at_square
Player: I should go to the square and finish the council talk with Arden, Steven, and everyone before coming back to the villagers here. [#hint=Quest 4: reach consensus at the council group]
=> END

~ arden_start
Arden: Hi there, kiddo! Our old canal has worked for decades—through nice rains and small dry spells, it always gave us water. But lately, climate change has made the weather go crazy: hotter heatwaves that last longer, less rain, and sudden big storms that break the canal. [#hint=Collect views on traditional wisdom]
- What did local people do to maintain the canal in the old days?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("arden_start", "maintenance", "curious")
	Arden: Villagers took turns repairing and watching over it year by year. The canal shares water evenly with every family, following the way mountains and valleys naturally let water flow. Our grandparents knew nature’s balance is easy to break—so they built it to save water when it rains, and let it out slowly when it’s dry.
- This old canal has protected this village for such a long time.
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("arden_start", "respect", "empathetic")
	Arden: Yes, it carries the wisdom of our ancestors. The canal shares water evenly with every family, following the way mountains and valleys naturally let water flow. Our grandparents knew nature’s balance is easy to break—so they built it to save water when it rains, and let it out slowly when it’s dry.
- Can the old canal still work well under climate change?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("arden_start", "climate", "action")
	Arden: It still works if we keep fixing it. The canal shares water evenly with every family, following the way mountains and valleys naturally let water flow. Our grandparents knew nature’s balance is easy to break—so they built it to save water when it rains, and let it out slowly when it’s dry.
Player: Why is the canal so smart, especially now that the climate is getting warmer?
Arden: The canal shares water evenly with every family, following the way mountains and valleys naturally let water flow. Our grandparents knew nature’s balance is easy to break—so they built it to save water when it rains, and let it out slowly when it’s dry. 
Arden: Water belongs to everyone, not just one person. When the climate is in trouble, sharing fairly and going with nature’s flow is the only way we can all live happily together.
Player: Are you scared that warmer weather will make water even harder to get?
Arden: We are—but being sad won’t fix it. The canal teaches us to be patient: fix it well, use water when the seasons say so, and help each other when the sun is super hot. [#hint=Traditional water use solution]
Arden: Climate change shows us we’re all connected to the earth; if we fight against nature, we’ll lose. Working with it, like the canal does, is the smart way our ancestors knew. [#hint=Traditional water use solution]
Arden: You can find Steven to explore his insights about the water savings.
do QuestState.mark_quest2_arden_done()
=> END

~ steven_start
Steven: I want to put in new water-saving tools—climate change is drying up our wells faster than ever, and we can’t just use old ways anymore!
- Where do we get these new water-saving tools?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("steven_start", "source", "curious")
	Steven: We can get them from outside the valley. Super easy! There are sprinklers that water plant roots directly, filters that clean dirty water, and tanks that catch rain when storms hit.
- You really worry about the village’s water shortage problem.
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("steven_start", "concern", "empathetic")
	Steven: I do, I hope no one in our village suffers from water shortage. Super easy! There are sprinklers that water plant roots directly, filters that clean dirty water, and tanks that catch rain when storms hit.
- Will installing these tools cost lots of manpower?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("steven_start", "effort", "action")
	Steven: We can learn together and finish installation step-by-step. Super easy! There are sprinklers that water plant roots directly, filters that clean dirty water, and tanks that catch rain when storms hit.
Player: Is this water-saving stuff easy to use, and can it really help with global warming?
Steven: Super easy! There are sprinklers that water plant roots directly, filters that clean dirty water, and tanks that catch rain when storms hit. [#hint=Collect modern water-saving solutions]
Steven: Since it’s getting hotter, we have less water to waste—these tools save half the water we used to lose. Saving every drop isn’t just a saying now [#hint=Collect modern water-saving solutions]
Steven: It’s something we need to do to get used to climate change. Our home’s future depends on using new ideas and being responsible [#hint=Collect modern water-saving solutions]
Player: Grandpa Arden wants to keep the old canal. Would you work with everyone to find a way that works for both?
Steven: Totally! Climate change doesn’t care if we use old or new things—what matters is we do something. The canal can save water, and my tools can too.[#hint=Recognize that tradition and modernity can be combined]
Steven: Putting them together will make us stronger against hot weather and weird rains. That’s the smart plan! [#hint=Recognize that tradition and modernity can be combined]
Steven: Also Aurora, she has some problem with her water resources, you will understand more about the problem if sympathizing to her ideas.
do QuestState.mark_quest2_steven_done()
=> END

~ aurora_start
Aurora: I wish I had clean water every day to wash my hands and water my flowers! But lately, the stream is either dry or muddy because climate change is making the weather so strange.
- Where do you plant your little flowers?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("aurora_start", "location", "curious")
	Aurora: They grow in my small family vegetable garden. Sometimes there’s no water for days—Mom can’t cook right, and our vegetable garden is almost dead. I’m so scared that one day, it will never rain again because the earth is too hot.
- It must be upsetting when your flowers cannot get clean water.
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("aurora_start", "feelings", "empathetic")
	Aurora: Yes, I feel sad seeing them becoming wilted. Sometimes there’s no water for days—Mom can’t cook right, and our vegetable garden is almost dead. I’m so scared that one day, it will never rain again because the earth is too hot.
- Is there any small way for kids to save water around here?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("aurora_start", "save_water", "action")
	Aurora: We can reuse water for watering plants. Sometimes there’s no water for days—Mom can’t cook right, and our vegetable garden is almost dead. I’m so scared that one day, it will never rain again because the earth is too hot.
Player: Is it really hard to get water now that the climate is getting worse?
Aurora: Sometimes there’s no water for days—Mom can’t cook right, and our vegetable garden is almost dead. I’m so scared that one day, it will never rain again because the earth is too hot.[#hint=Collect real children’s needs]
Player: Don’t worry, we’ll find a way together. Climate change is a big problem, but if we all help each other, we can beat it.
Aurora: Yay! Thank you! Let’s cherish every drop of water, fix the canal, and use the new tools.  [#hint=Children’s consensus on water conservation]
Aurora: If we take good care of water, water will take care of us—even when the climate is bad. Let’s keep the stream clean forever, for us and all the little animals that drink from it!  [#hint=Children’s consensus on water conservation]
do QuestState.mark_quest2_aurora_done()
=> END

~ arden_steven_villagers_start
- Both ideas are valuable. Can we consider all opinions together?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("arden_steven_villagers_start", "consider", "curious")
- Everyone hopes the village can have enough water. Let’s listen to each other.
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("arden_steven_villagers_start", "listen", "empathetic")
- Let’s try to find one solution that works for all villagers.
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("arden_steven_villagers_start", "solution", "action")
Player: Grandpa Arden, Steven, and everyone, let’s work together! When encountering problems, discussing them well can help you understand each others perspective and find a solution together  [#hint=Guide negotiation and communication]
Arden: I want to repair the traditional canal to ensure fairness.
Steven: I want to install water-saving equipment to save water efficiently.
Player: Then let’s do both! Repair the canal to ensure fairness, install equipment to save water, and make a water use pact for everyone to  agree to and take care of together. Is that okay?  [#hint=Propose an integrated solution]
Arden: This method is good! I agree!
Steven: I agree too!
Villagers: We all agree! [#hint=Reach full consensus]
do QuestState.mark_quest4_complete()
=> END

~ villagers_quest5
- What kinds of rubbish are blocking the inside of the canal?
