~ matt_ch2_start
Matt: The sea has its own rules. When the tide rises and falls, we must follow them.
- What will happen if we ignore the tide?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("matt_ch2_start", "risk", "curious")
	Matt: We may put ourselves in danger. The sea gives us food. We must respect and take good care of it, so that it can nourish us forever. Humans and nature must get along well.
- Fishermen have lived with the tide for a very long time, right?
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("matt_ch2_start", "tradition", "empathetic")
	Matt: Yes, it is the wisdom passed down by our ancestors. The sea gives us food. We must respect and take good care of it, so that it can nourish us forever. Humans and nature must get along well.
- So we should learn these rules before we go fishing.
	do DialogueChoices.record_choice("matt_ch2_start", "learn", "action")
	Matt: Exactly. We cannot take from the sea without limits. The sea gives us food. We must respect and take good care of it, so that it can nourish us forever. Humans and nature must get along well.
Player: Why should we follow the rules of the sea?
Matt: The sea gives us food. We must respect and take good care of it, so that it can nourish us forever. Humans and nature must get along well [#hint=learn traditional marine ecological knowledge]
do QuestState.mark_chapter2_quest1_matt_done()
=> END

~ matt_ch2_repeat
Matt: Respect the tide and the sea will keep giving. Talk to Kai next for Quest 3.
=> END

~ kai_ch2_start
Kai: I want to use new methods: ecological breeding, regular fishing moratorium, and low-carbon fishing.
Player: Can traditional experience and innovation work together?
Kai: Yes. Let the small fish grow up before catching; then there will always be fish in the sea.
do QuestState.mark_chapter2_quest1_kai_done()
=> END

~ kai_ch2_repeat
Kai: Ecological breeding and fishing moratoriums can work with tradition. Finish Quest 2 by speaking with Matt at the wharf.
